nitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=-t-2,\\,b=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n-t-2=1.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-t & =3 \\\\ t & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549009 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bc56c0e-abb3-5e53-818e-6aa51cc4af57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548439} {:answer/id #uuid "36c25fee-f58b-5409-a5a6-477628433eda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548463} {:answer/id #uuid "5ade53e2-0afe-522f-a54b-d5db209e2043" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548522} {:answer/id #uuid "6e3dbfdf-aa6d-5049-8e76-3fb7df01f4ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548560} {:answer/id #uuid "7e1a1e15-d44d-54e8-a331-6ddc82706537" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548595}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548595} :answer-field/id #uuid "e27fc349-c24d-5039-a135-0c2dd2d434ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548967}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "61992611-6e48-5a2e-9a4c-b280b994c51d" :question/math-academy-id "q-288561" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(t+4)x=2x\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $t?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=t+4,\\,b=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\nt+4=2.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\nt=-2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549054 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eab2015-3700-56bf-ac92-fd0a94f661e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548387} {:answer/id #uuid "598ab84f-b8ec-5739-8cb0-fcbd36abfb06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548519} {:answer/id #uuid "96f6e7b6-efcd-5a03-89c8-76e8676edae9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548648} {:answer/id #uuid "987b69a0-afb9-52c1-b363-d34b352bfb3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548656} {:answer/id #uuid "f71c8c9c-cd96-5e6e-b046-0e4cdbb2a210" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548847}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548656} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e0b4382-89cc-5b45-b568-d6f0542bad8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d6ec3c46-2316-5f98-be70-c232b5719be1" :question/math-academy-id "q-288560" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(r-7)x=-x\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $r?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=r-7,\\,b=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\nr-7=-1.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\nr=6.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553489 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48c2e38d-b9ae-5469-80da-9fbdd71de470" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553020} {:answer/id #uuid "4c2cbe8d-3e4b-5384-8b16-725c4463dd05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553034} {:answer/id #uuid "9c93cb76-4c56-5d96-8b69-8368ebaaeef1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553187} {:answer/id #uuid "c2d7c1ac-66b2-5bec-8e54-ba134d4a8328" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553257} {:answer/id #uuid "f9b558ea-14a4-554d-a74d-bad2d525c660" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553363}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553034} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b7c28ee-4474-52da-a1df-71e406689457" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553417}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "021c858c-ff29-5406-946e-12cf5966af05" :question/math-academy-id "q-288441" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n7mx=-14x\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $m?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=7m,\\,b=-14.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n7m=-14.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $m,$ we get\n\n$$\nm=-2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555761 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b03681d2-3f8d-5d5a-b665-08eb5334df77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555422}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186555422} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8685c5a-c976-5c4d-ba32-65fdf5654aca" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186555734}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "06a0435e-893d-5476-8004-8e6faf0aa039" :question/math-academy-id "q-288569" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(4r-6)x=22x\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $r?$\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=4r-6,\\,b=22.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n4r-6=22.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4r & =28 \\\\ r & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving For an Unknown Parameter"} :step/id #uuid "1e04eac3-4a93-5d72-9a90-2b1099628885" :step/math-academy-id 476194 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111683}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111683 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111819 :tutorial/content "Each of the following equations has infinitely many solutions:\n\n$$\n2x + 1 = 2x + 1, \\qquad \\dfrac13x -2 = \\dfrac13x -2, \\qquad 1-x = 1 - x\n$$\n\nMore generally, suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax + b = cx + d,\n$$\n\nwhere $a, b, c,$ and $d$ are constants. Then, this equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na = c\\quad \\text{and}\\quad b = d.\n$$\n\nNote that if we substitute $c=a$ and $d=b$ into this equation, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax+b & =cx+d \\\\ ax+b & =(a)x+(b) \\\\ ax+b & =ax+b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying this equation by subtracting $ax$ from both sides of the equation gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax+b & =ax+b \\\\ ax+b-ax & =ax+b-ax \\\\ b & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation, $b = b,$ is always true. Therefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions, and any real value of $x$ is a solution." :tutorial/id #uuid "7498a4f8-36b5-5d1d-b953-893581bfa9aa" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13430 :tutorial/title "Conditions for a General Linear Equation To Have Infinitely Many Solutions"} :step/id #uuid "1d109dfd-fdee-5718-a3d2-a9a96f41ffd0" :step/math-academy-id 476197 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111684}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111684 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111817 :tutorial/content "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(s - 2)x + t = 6x + 9,\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ and $t$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, how can we find the values of $s$ and $t?$\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax+b = cx+d{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(s-2)}_{\\large a} \\ x + \\underbrace{t}_{\\large b} = \\underbrace{6}_{\\large c} \\ x + \\underbrace{9}_{\\large d}\n$$\n\nWe know that an equation of the form $ax+b = cx+d$ has infinitely many solutions when $a=c$ and $b=d.$\n\nSo, to determine the values of $s$ and $t$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n({\\color{blue}{{s - 2}}})x + {\\color{red}{{t}}} = {\\color{blue}{{6}}}x + {\\color{red}{{9}}}\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we **equate the coefficients** of $x,$ and we **equate the constants:**\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{{s - 2}}} = {\\color{blue}{{6}}}.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $s,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{{t}}} = {\\color{red}{{9}}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $s = 8$ and $t=9,$ our original equation has infinitely many solutions." :tutorial/id #uuid "dc5c5f3e-0731-5ab3-8afd-a2cf24228db1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13403 :tutorial/title "Problems Involving Unknown Parameters in General Linear Equations"} :step/id #uuid "01172b4e-9bb6-512f-9e14-1b2ad76e2a6f" :step/math-academy-id 476198 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111685}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111685 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110721 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110371 :question/id #uuid "6eeec12a-5da8-5113-916a-58f4ca807207" :question/math-academy-id "e-18852" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(k+4)x + 3r = 6x + 12\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ and $r$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $k+r?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax + b = cx + d\n$$\n\nwhere $a, b, c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na = c\\quad \\text{and}\\quad b = d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $k$ and $r$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n(k+4)x + 3r = 6x + 12\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\nk + 4 = 6.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we have\n\n$$\n3r = 12.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $k = 2$ and $r = 4,$ we have\n\n$$\nk + r = 2+4 = 6.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b1fd794-f42b-5316-80e9-0698cb1f8b87" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477026 :question-generator/id #uuid "a8a3fd9e-9b0f-54b0-b19c-26e749314cab" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186110721.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111136 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2918787-6369-5281-b839-56303be60818" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108557} {:answer/id #uuid "d22c7204-b52f-5b6a-ad06-733bfdfad3e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108558} {:answer/id #uuid "e529c4c3-c68a-5582-8d90-3a6410fd1ff0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108559} {:answer/id #uuid "074e8b23-4661-5096-bb58-43d425517222" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108560} {:answer/id #uuid "558a10b7-3a92-55d7-9829-746619fff524" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108561}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c9db9fdf-89d7-5da6-8efa-fb1d0de77d4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108556}] :question/id #uuid "46a7b671-3423-5c64-a7e7-5db3d2823ec3" :question/math-academy-id "q-287978" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n$(p - 3)x + q = 4x + 1$\nwhere $p$ and $q$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p + q$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111137 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "e1aa3a94-3d74-527f-9f2b-4344ff0ea098" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108562}] :question/id #uuid "680bddd9-26ae-52c5-946c-8e72aafff50a" :question/math-academy-id "q-287985" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n$(p - 5)x + 2q = 2x + 6$\nwhere $p$ and $q$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p + q$?$p + q =$\n{{field-1}}"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297194 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11383c9f-6bca-51a0-bf87-839bec637ba1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295365}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295365} :answer-field/id #uuid "b78bfb02-4c20-5a75-811c-305086ea1983" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297016}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "73256cc5-b18c-5ffc-bb1b-9018e84f6eb6" :question/math-academy-id "q-288583" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n2tx+(1-m)=6x+4\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ and $m$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $t+m?$\n\n$t+m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $t$ and $m$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n2tx+(1-m)=6x+4\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n2t=6.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\nt=3.\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we get\n\n$$\n1-m=4.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $m,$ we get\n\n$$\nm=-3.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $t=3$ and $m=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\nt+m=3+(-3)=0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297195 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe4c39bf-4560-5825-a22a-76a5df230cbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296621}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296621} :answer-field/id #uuid "58355121-2d5d-5aa8-91c2-79f41befa005" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296811}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c1e37e75-7d0c-5923-8a40-5f706dc21da1" :question/math-academy-id "q-288584" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(-s+3)x+2t=x+6\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ and $t$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $s+t?$\n\n$s+t=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $s$ and $t$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n(-s+3)x+2t=x+6\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n-s+3=1.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $s,$ we get\n\n$$\ns=2.\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we have\n\n$$\n2t=6.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\nt=3.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $s=2$ and $t=3,$ we have\n\n$$\ns+t=2+3=5.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297198 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26dea449-5daa-571d-bc24-21b16885571b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295478} {:answer/id #uuid "46ff9640-80f1-5703-b7a0-6219bb604868" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295672} {:answer/id #uuid "7f368c30-e2fd-5534-8546-adacd3bdc62c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295955} {:answer/id #uuid "bb0e5e07-5208-59c1-8031-79b63eb57a08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296248} {:answer/id #uuid "e8efa7fd-e32d-5724-96bb-c481dd8ea2c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296507}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295672} :answer-field/id #uuid "049c5b1b-3107-5777-ad7f-5a1a5ac925c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296646}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "afc322c5-285c-5560-a114-d1da26bf18af" :question/math-academy-id "q-288622" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(m+2)x+5n=7x+15\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ and $n$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $m+n?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $m$ and $n$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n(m+2)x+5n=7x+15\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\nm+2=7.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $m,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we have\n\n$$\n5n=15.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $n,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $m=5$ and $n=3,$ we have\n\n$$\nm+n=5+3=8.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297199 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16a9032f-3b49-520d-8dd2-f9561ab549a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295402} {:answer/id #uuid "408f08fe-9474-5041-b518-50e39558bff0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295635} {:answer/id #uuid "40aa1a16-e6d9-5660-9646-91aa012afb7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295636} {:answer/id #uuid "4376d57c-3e21-5f7a-9e08-da1e663ee90a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295647} {:answer/id #uuid "f4e2b538-9abc-5649-871f-12082724e81b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296563}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296563} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7f50aa1-8f5f-55b0-9f35-0014c59522a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296988}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "48147a3d-8103-52a6-b2f5-5907d423b5d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-288623" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n2kx+r=9x+6\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ and $r$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $k+r?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $k$ and $r$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n2kx+r=9x+6\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n2k=9.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we have\n\n$$\nr=6.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $k=\\frac{9}{2}$ and $r=6,$ we have\n\n$$\nk+r=\\frac{9}{2}+6=\\frac{21}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297200 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28da790f-20c9-5875-b417-1be764fa2aeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295496} {:answer/id #uuid "8db21277-c307-578a-8ebf-d5c8733da1ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296020} {:answer/id #uuid "c24c121c-9aae-5ae4-989f-173c011f7cdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296294} {:answer/id #uuid "c7c8cfa6-04bf-59ad-b50b-09698209ddbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296316} {:answer/id #uuid "e7994d65-33f9-5bec-9e19-0969a17a2b2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296497}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296020} :answer-field/id #uuid "a71ebbc6-abec-50c8-b8f1-d671ed8c0cf9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296986}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9f55693d-3dd0-5310-a538-5dcb7f69af97" :question/math-academy-id "q-288627" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{p}{2}x+q=4x+20\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ and $q$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p+q?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $p$ and $q$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{p}{2}x+q=4x+20\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{p}{2}=4.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we get\n\n$$\nq=20.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $p=8$ and $q=20,$ we have\n\n$$\np+q=8+20=28.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477006 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f507488-4595-522d-987d-9cb1830b28d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477012} {:answer/id #uuid "809ccd9d-fcfa-5f07-b35d-ba36d56528a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477013} {:answer/id #uuid "85a2d72a-110b-5ef3-91a7-6c6ea19a612f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "88" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477014} {:answer/id #uuid "b5e87be2-7f84-5050-8450-0a88afe51a84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477017} {:answer/id #uuid "e45d5b04-882a-57f9-a42e-18c402ad7f7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477024}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477012} :answer-field/id #uuid "fecfe7d0-ddbf-5842-ad10-352285c2f4d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477025}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "436cab40-c84e-51f6-a518-ef754e1d23e8" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(m+7)x+8n=9x+88\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ and $n$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $m+n?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $m$ and $n$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n(m+7)x+8n=9x+88\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\nm+7=9.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $m,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we have\n\n$$\n8n=88.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $n,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $m=2$ and $n=11,$ we have\n\n$$\nm+n=2+11=13.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477007 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e054d18f-0abd-534e-af79-b4becb7e8af4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477023}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477023} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf404ba6-be51-5d74-9701-f28660bc675d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477019}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "475d1245-f209-5a1a-94d0-b61255568fb1" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(-s+16)x+2t=7x+12\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ and $t$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $s+t?$\n\n$s+t=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $s$ and $t$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n(-s+16)x+2t=7x+12\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n-s+16=7.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $s,$ we get\n\n$$\ns=9.\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we have\n\n$$\n2t=12.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\nt=6.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $s=9$ and $t=6,$ we have\n\n$$\ns+t=9+6=15.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477016 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c305d1ca-2a7e-5395-b01d-7f73d8b68b81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477020}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477020} :answer-field/id #uuid "94e8a50e-ea95-52e9-9247-89cc6599449e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "af3151d7-d56f-5c03-a92e-cf11481af995" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(-s+15)x+4t=3x+4\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ and $t$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $s+t?$\n\n$s+t=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $s$ and $t$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n(-s+15)x+4t=3x+4\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n-s+15=3.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $s,$ we get\n\n$$\ns=12.\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we have\n\n$$\n4t=4.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\nt=1.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $s=12$ and $t=1,$ we have\n\n$$\ns+t=12+1=13.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477018 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e96ccbc-fae0-58bf-a260-8810e64e49c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477003} {:answer/id #uuid "6135c2cd-ed41-554f-a365-6407044708b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477008} {:answer/id #uuid "64867657-b42b-5877-a7b8-d5f08508e6f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477009} {:answer/id #uuid "7795fc96-bada-5d47-80e4-bcc20f479e5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477011} {:answer/id #uuid "da276f27-55cf-5a27-b424-c537ddd7a3e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477022}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477003} :answer-field/id #uuid "1685aded-b577-5ed8-90cf-3a8f75f8adf6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bae891b2-2044-5361-b2ef-88211b0b3d9f" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(m+8)x+8n=19x+48\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ and $n$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $m+n?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $m$ and $n$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n(m+8)x+8n=19x+48\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\nm+8=19.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $m,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we have\n\n$$\n8n=48.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $n,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $m=11$ and $n=6,$ we have\n\n$$\nm+n=11+6=17.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477021 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29420e57-07ec-566d-b584-32e9a65019be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477005}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186477005} :answer-field/id #uuid "74d8a1c6-b3bb-5405-b5c5-2de709852649" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477010}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d2ad0a7a-e832-5a60-bb09-72e5ecf114bf" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n5tx+(1-m)=55x+4\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ and $m$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $t+m?$\n\n$t+m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $t$ and $m$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n5tx+(1-m)=55x+4\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n5t=55.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\nt=11.\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we get\n\n$$\n1-m=4.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $m,$ we get\n\n$$\nm=-3.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $t=11$ and $m=-3,$ we have\n\n$$\nt+m=11+(-3)=8.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492013 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "024ebb29-e554-55b8-8568-3de971cf842b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491452} {:answer/id #uuid "0b409fa8-7fa2-5333-b719-1bf09e74f752" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491476} {:answer/id #uuid "52608292-aa25-53a5-a80a-a1687fff019e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491611} {:answer/id #uuid "e4026fca-c4a4-58f0-adbe-16c380971427" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491844} {:answer/id #uuid "f0569689-8bdd-5f6e-84ed-219724f09b06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491865}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491844} :answer-field/id #uuid "9275af45-98fb-5d13-b62c-71ff39cdad22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4fff83f5-94fb-54ad-8709-ecc3c59291a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-288574" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(k+2)x+r=5x+3\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ and $r$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $k+r?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $k$ and $r$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n(k+2)x+r=5x+3\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\nk+2=5.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\nk=3.\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we get\n\n$$\nr=3.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $k=3$ and $r=3,$ we have\n\n$$\nk+r=3+3=6.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a General Equation With Infinitely Many Solutions For Unknown Parameters"} :step/id #uuid "c8fdb7d5-63c2-55ac-a293-d9536babfe47" :step/math-academy-id 476199}] :activity/title "Linear Equations With Infinitely Many Solutions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110810} {:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549131} :activity/id #uuid "53e407aa-b874-5ab8-ad52-b6329fa3b408" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049310} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549090 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297195 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe4c39bf-4560-5825-a22a-76a5df230cbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296621}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296621} :answer-field/id #uuid "58355121-2d5d-5aa8-91c2-79f41befa005" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296811}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c1e37e75-7d0c-5923-8a40-5f706dc21da1" :question/math-academy-id "q-288584" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(-s+3)x+2t=x+6\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ and $t$ are constants. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $s+t?$\n\n$s+t=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax+b=cx+d\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are constants. This equation has infinitely many solutions if \n\n$$\na=c\\,\\text{and}\\,b=d.\n$$\n\nTo determine the values of $s$ and $t$ such that the equation\n\n$$\n(-s+3)x+2t=x+6\n$$\n\nhas infinitely many solutions, we equate the coefficients of $x,$ and we equate the constants:\n\n- Equating the coefficients of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n-s+3=1.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $s,$ we get\n\n$$\ns=2.\n$$\n\n- Equating the constants, we have\n\n$$\n2t=6.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\nt=3.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $s=2$ and $t=3,$ we have\n\n$$\ns+t=2+3=5.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3b9efce2-cc34-5966-9ee7-777169e666e6"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549108 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549054 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eab2015-3700-56bf-ac92-fd0a94f661e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548387} {:answer/id #uuid "598ab84f-b8ec-5739-8cb0-fcbd36abfb06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548519} {:answer/id #uuid "96f6e7b6-efcd-5a03-89c8-76e8676edae9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548648} {:answer/id #uuid "987b69a0-afb9-52c1-b363-d34b352bfb3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548656} {:answer/id #uuid "f71c8c9c-cd96-5e6e-b046-0e4cdbb2a210" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548847}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548656} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e0b4382-89cc-5b45-b568-d6f0542bad8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d6ec3c46-2316-5f98-be70-c232b5719be1" :question/math-academy-id "q-288560" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(r-7)x=-x\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $r?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=r-7,\\,b=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\nr-7=-1.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\nr=6.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "70e3dbbc-f265-58ec-abc4-fa61aa670f9a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549129}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549129 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549009 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bc56c0e-abb3-5e53-818e-6aa51cc4af57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548439} {:answer/id #uuid "36c25fee-f58b-5409-a5a6-477628433eda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548463} {:answer/id #uuid "5ade53e2-0afe-522f-a54b-d5db209e2043" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548522} {:answer/id #uuid "6e3dbfdf-aa6d-5049-8e76-3fb7df01f4ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548560} {:answer/id #uuid "7e1a1e15-d44d-54e8-a331-6ddc82706537" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548595}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548595} :answer-field/id #uuid "e27fc349-c24d-5039-a135-0c2dd2d434ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548967}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "61992611-6e48-5a2e-9a4c-b280b994c51d" :question/math-academy-id "q-288561" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(t+4)x=2x\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $t?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=t+4,\\,b=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\nt+4=2.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $t,$ we get\n\n$$\nt=-2.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9c38efa4-70b9-572d-aabd-a33044024fc7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549090}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549131 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111135 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14a449b2-0875-581b-a2ec-8813a227c1de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295383} {:answer/id #uuid "2d6f283f-3592-5248-baf7-ca782b7d8c9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295530} {:answer/id #uuid "4009ef03-e229-5324-9dbd-214305b60cd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295633} {:answer/id #uuid "4dfca85a-c698-5e36-ba67-02a55f783f85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295715} {:answer/id #uuid "d199324f-9733-59f2-82cf-8e4f45ae01ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296371}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295383} :answer-field/id #uuid "1739b830-13d4-586f-9761-4372557e83e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296679}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "435ae769-5577-5924-a2b3-9f23f7e0ccc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-287971" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(p-3)x=4x\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=p-3,\\,b=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\np-3=4.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\np=7.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a60530a2-2052-544d-a2a2-f6b827880aca" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549108}}] :activity/title "Linear Equations With Infinitely Many Solutions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548342}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186049310 :topic/difficulty 0.7310479111284743 :topic/id #uuid "eb6655db-68e9-5ef3-bf7a-81223e92dac0" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110719 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110372 :question/id #uuid "6355ac1e-bb4d-5133-9917-04dfb2bc5a0d" :question/math-academy-id "e-18854" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nI. $4x + 1 = 4x + 1$\n\nII. $-3x = -4x + 6$\n\nIII. $2x + 7 = 2x - 1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+1 & =4x+1 \\\\ 4x+1-4x & =4x+1-4x \\\\ 1 & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $4x + 1 = 4x + 1$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x & =-4x+6 \\\\ -3x+4x & =-4x+6+4x \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $-3x = -4x + 6$ has the unique solution $x = 6.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+7 & =2x-1 \\\\ 2x+7-2x & =2x-1-2x \\\\ 7 & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff4e9141-5743-55f1-a8be-3e28cb047eca" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048213}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476103 :question-generator/id #uuid "393aec7c-08a3-58cd-ac4d-3705704af62b" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186110719.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111133 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ec6698b-966d-5e31-b760-5faeba4367be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438040} {:answer/id #uuid "ce68107b-c73c-5910-8bb8-b2dd40c29da8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438041}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438040} :answer-field/id #uuid "15760992-2dd2-5d43-b2a8-1b987c48791a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439049} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5f4a2b7-9944-5e1c-9ff0-88248a565319" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438042} {:answer/id #uuid "46c6e30e-5409-5621-86de-fccef2076faa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438043}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438043} :answer-field/id #uuid "73a78852-4a44-54f0-a170-8df3bd3710e3" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439050} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95fb5bb7-a16c-591d-9bc5-3cc80a4f1c75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438044} {:answer/id #uuid "40143427-1f60-591b-865b-52bd73399fa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438045}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438044} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7b0dbf4-aa8c-5f41-bd3d-257a796c46ed" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439051}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "71c3d793-6b55-5392-ac94-1149b022497f" :question/math-academy-id "q-287966" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $2x=x+1$\n- **II.** $2x+1=2x+1$\n- **III.** $2x+1=2x+3$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =x+1 \\\\ 2x-x & =x+1-x \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $2x=x+1$ has the unique solution $x=1.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+1 & =2x+1 \\\\ 2x+1-2x & =2x+1-2x \\\\ 1 & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $2x+1=2x+1$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+1 & =2x+3 \\\\ 2x+1-2x & =2x+3-2x \\\\ 1 & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111138 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "801c1341-5837-5b3c-bdca-f820a9ee8871" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438076} {:answer/id #uuid "48e9078a-e6f9-59fb-af05-9029d3a48e04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438077}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438076} :answer-field/id #uuid "e865206f-261e-52b1-ae6e-68e8b33c31b3" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439067} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d05df7f-dec5-59ce-b63b-c00fa9f8a672" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438078} {:answer/id #uuid "36e2ee78-8b01-5053-aa94-5d0e687cddc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438079}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438079} :answer-field/id #uuid "e502be4e-9bbd-5dbb-bb41-0769be49d17b" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439068} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a8ec9a73-6fbc-5213-bad0-073cc49fb549" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438080} {:answer/id #uuid "29a60663-a01a-5898-a8b1-97b46dadd573" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438081}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438080} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bcded34-504d-5f90-a10a-87a266602706" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439069}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6b24019f-f592-514c-8874-109e97014d8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-288419" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $3x=2x+5$\n- **II.** $4x-2=4x-2$\n- **III.** $5x+3=5x+1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =2x+5 \\\\ 3x-2x & =2x+5-2x \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $3x=2x+5$ has the unique solution $x=5.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-2 & =4x-2 \\\\ 4x-2-4x & =4x-2-4x \\\\ -2 & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $4x-2=4x-2$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+3 & =5x+1 \\\\ 5x+3-5x & =5x+1-5x \\\\ 3 & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297181 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51b73290-8884-52b0-bea9-586ed1106191" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438082} {:answer/id #uuid "84594a5c-0472-579f-8c56-3c27f2aa0b2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438083}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438082} :answer-field/id #uuid "d237ec26-3cf6-575e-bb01-acccb260545f" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439070} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6aa4deae-76c0-5bf7-a4af-1158715b25d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438084} {:answer/id #uuid "e6ba602a-8c17-5e28-b8db-32b4e57396a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438085}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438084} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0236bbe-ed0c-5681-b717-8d06a5afcad6" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439071} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18b5cb76-d351-5058-a991-9afc4efc993e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438086} {:answer/id #uuid "b1790360-3d45-5107-959e-c1c762336309" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438087}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438086} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae99ebca-b507-5034-9f94-d990616ca1d6" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439072}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d5426119-4ea0-5c84-bb6c-cf460c622db0" :question/math-academy-id "q-288425" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $-3x+2=-3x+7$\n- **II.** $6x+1=6x+5$\n- **III.** $7x-4=6x-6$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x+2 & =-3x+7 \\\\ -3x+2+3x & =-3x+7+3x \\\\ 2 & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+1 & =6x+5 \\\\ 6x+1-6x & =6x+5-6x \\\\ 1 & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x-4 & =6x-6 \\\\ 7x-4-6x & =6x-6-6x \\\\ x-4 & =-6 \\\\ x-4+4 & =-6+4 \\\\ x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $7x-4=6x-6$ has the unique solution $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297182 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86bc7e66-5bdd-5da7-b6ee-6b1a37c5ae9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438088} {:answer/id #uuid "1dc3c549-6f72-5c75-9824-c4564938c74a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438089}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438088} :answer-field/id #uuid "01d95513-0985-54ed-85b5-aee1f0de0676" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439073} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93091e42-4e1d-50af-874b-052e5b4ccf7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438090} {:answer/id #uuid "831cc9b8-0274-570c-a213-da0beeeda4c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438091}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438090} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ccfd62c-12fc-5d4f-a40e-e4d63caedad3" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439074} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1844e447-1273-5f4a-9121-461fc7343eef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438092} {:answer/id #uuid "fbfbb123-d421-50c5-bb94-58c01fc307f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438093}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438093} :answer-field/id #uuid "a73ad185-931c-5fc2-9419-95cfddabcee6" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439075}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "31ef6d07-7ebc-5067-b0a5-3f361bec8d1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-288427" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $2x-5=x-2$\n- **II.** $6x+1=6x+9$\n- **III.** $-4x+3=-4x+3$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-5 & =x-2 \\\\ 2x-5-x & =x-2-x \\\\ x-5 & =-2 \\\\ x-5+5 & =-2+5 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $2x-5=x-2$ has the unique solution $x=3.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x+1 & =6x+9 \\\\ 6x+1-6x & =6x+9-6x \\\\ 1 & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x+3 & =-4x+3 \\\\ -4x+3+4x & =-4x+3+4x \\\\ 3 & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $-4x+3=-4x+3$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297196 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f505619a-0f8f-5b06-bcd1-9ed29f3a46e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438094} {:answer/id #uuid "800b7f50-dd11-50d4-910a-43bdf1c22e22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438095}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438094} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d40aab5-2a51-5f28-9398-9e1f95aef724" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439076} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35bdc9da-af4c-51a3-977a-aab317bf1688" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438096} {:answer/id #uuid "b473c870-043e-51e2-b1e7-8129f88a2f5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438097}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438096} :answer-field/id #uuid "f63c5f3b-e9f0-5d44-b9b5-69d5b9503e16" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439077} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca3aa2f5-1e64-5717-a73e-d38a34cd3c61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438098} {:answer/id #uuid "14f6741b-26e8-5eea-b15d-fa64a12ad311" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438099}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438099} :answer-field/id #uuid "6515f165-1d78-588e-8621-8c95e0aa4812" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "43eccfac-bd80-5f7b-abff-3250eee5dc70" :question/math-academy-id "q-288591" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $3x+2=4x+1$\n- **II.** $2x-5=3x-3$\n- **III.** $5x-4=5x-4$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2 & =4x+1 \\\\ 3x+2-3x & =4x+1-3x \\\\ 2 & =x+1 \\\\ 2-1 & =x+1-1 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $3x+2=4x+1$ has the unique solution $x=1.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-5 & =3x-3 \\\\ 2x-5-2x & =3x-3-2x \\\\ -5 & =x-3 \\\\ -5+3 & =x-3+3 \\\\ x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $2x-5=3x-3$ has the unique solution $x=-2.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-4 & =5x-4 \\\\ 5x-4-5x & =5x-4-5x \\\\ -4 & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $5x-4=5x-4$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492000 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1471cbdf-bc97-5a46-8981-7245c7bab5e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491495} {:answer/id #uuid "48935b84-81f3-595a-95e3-e1292918d6c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491590} {:answer/id #uuid "6e935aa1-98de-58bc-8f29-612955963de6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491653} {:answer/id #uuid "7b157d25-3f29-59c7-994c-6b0e54448964" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491670} {:answer/id #uuid "8b47ef80-7919-5404-a1ab-a8ded7329dca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491692}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491692} :answer-field/id #uuid "e746e60c-472a-557f-8568-6d42ae2542ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491975}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1d18683e-9cdc-5535-b6a1-ea077ac9eff1" :question/math-academy-id "q-288426" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nI. $4x+2=4x+2$\n\nII. $-2x-1=-2x-1$\n\nIII. $3x+5=3x+9$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+2 & =4x+2 \\\\ 4x+2-4x & =4x+2-4x \\\\ 2 & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $4x+2=4x+2$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x-1 & =-2x-1 \\\\ -2x-1+2x & =-2x-1+2x \\\\ -1 & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $-2x-1=-2x-1$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+5 & =3x+9 \\\\ 3x+5-3x & =3x+9-3x \\\\ 5 & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492056 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "122da567-888b-521d-ab93-2bdadd7b97a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491491} {:answer/id #uuid "2c757179-64e0-523b-afeb-d5bc0ad04d2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491543} {:answer/id #uuid "93ecc79a-c63b-50da-a503-9b6d8ce7074b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491705} {:answer/id #uuid "ab75275b-5761-5361-ae34-c628459ca7b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491752} {:answer/id #uuid "cfb65399-285a-555c-b2e6-5f5e24676387" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491807}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491543} :answer-field/id #uuid "884fd6c3-8303-53d8-9178-3d82c6fc0a29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491937}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cc8f5f5a-7e03-528b-a06c-bf3c3858a8c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-288431" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nI. $3x+4=3x+4$\n\nII. $x-5=x-2$\n\nIII. $2x+3=x+3$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+4 & =3x+4 \\\\ 3x+4-3x & =3x+4-3x \\\\ 4 & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $3x+4=3x+4$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-5 & =x-2 \\\\ x-5-x & =x-2-x \\\\ -5 & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $x.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+3 & =x+3 \\\\ 2x+3-x & =x+3-x \\\\ x+3 & =3 \\\\ x+3-3 & =3-3 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $2x+3=x+3$ has the unique solution $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations With Infinitely Many Solutions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110720 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110368 :question/id #uuid "4a8f8deb-2ff1-56d2-888a-3e51eb805bc4" :question/math-academy-id "e-17212" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(5s + 4)x = 9x\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $s?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax = bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a = b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax = bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na = 5s + 4, \\qquad b = 9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n5s + 4 = 9.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $s,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5s & =5 \\\\ s & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6cd2f04-e0d5-5df0-8e74-ea737501cdf8" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476197 :question-generator/id #uuid "c05f43b0-71c3-5af5-bc14-8a813a2da1af" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186110720.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111134 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16ca1cf7-eae8-588f-b62e-92e2eed7ae06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295403} {:answer/id #uuid "5f7c70a7-d1b8-56a0-9961-6101a0fea82a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295813} {:answer/id #uuid "89099718-058c-53aa-ac2e-3dc7b59c4b5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296004} {:answer/id #uuid "a1cb3a02-6977-565b-8c6f-64a04e91a8d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296129} {:answer/id #uuid "fcf45a98-a317-5ebe-a0ee-d58863e54e01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296613}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295403} :answer-field/id #uuid "78f9540e-726b-5906-87cd-68bbcee4ccc9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "573bc8f1-012d-5d7e-834a-c6ec17bb0b63" :question/math-academy-id "q-287970" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n2px=3x\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=2p,\\,b=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n2p=3.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\np=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111135 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14a449b2-0875-581b-a2ec-8813a227c1de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295383} {:answer/id #uuid "2d6f283f-3592-5248-baf7-ca782b7d8c9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295530} {:answer/id #uuid "4009ef03-e229-5324-9dbd-214305b60cd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295633} {:answer/id #uuid "4dfca85a-c698-5e36-ba67-02a55f783f85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295715} {:answer/id #uuid "d199324f-9733-59f2-82cf-8e4f45ae01ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296371}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295383} :answer-field/id #uuid "1739b830-13d4-586f-9761-4372557e83e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296679}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "435ae769-5577-5924-a2b3-9f23f7e0ccc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-287971" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(p-3)x=4x\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=p-3,\\,b=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\np-3=4.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\np=7.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297170 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b30a8cc-6928-5b29-8e9d-969094b2881b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295513}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295513} :answer-field/id #uuid "f87dd3f0-9ada-5f78-bd01-d45ae62ce046" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297144}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e508284d-0585-5c0f-a542-d91d66f78ddf" :question/math-academy-id "q-287975" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n(2p-3)x=5x\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=2p-3,\\,b=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n2p-3=5.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2p & =8 \\\\ p & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297183 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45491d55-84a5-5641-85ee-02ca1a1e90b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295660} {:answer/id #uuid "7b982f8e-1429-5175-9e47-06c33c917451" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295944} {:answer/id #uuid "9130ffb1-ff3d-56f6-b44a-a46263f888e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296047} {:answer/id #uuid "9540e0f1-3b23-5210-976d-85a05ecc13df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296063} {:answer/id #uuid "f61f19b9-1bc9-5327-81ed-c1e60a23106f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296576}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186296063} :answer-field/id #uuid "057a3936-10b0-5e7b-83aa-558ff78363fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296650}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "556d6e5b-c151-55e9-9606-63f27cad6bd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-288436" :question/problem "Consider the equation\n\n$$\n5px=10x\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has infinitely many solutions, what is the value of $p?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=bx\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. If $a=b,$ then the equation has infinitely many solutions.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=bx.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=5p,\\,b=10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has infinitely many solutions if\n\n$$\n5p=10.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\np=2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297184 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26fe00c2-ff7d-5b62-96ab-0af209fd24e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/